ore":1,"confidence":"high","evidence":["catalog text contains \"essay\"","catalog text contains \"essays\"","catalog text contains \"criticism\"","literature/criticism subject or topic"]},{"id":"biographical","score":0.58,"confidence":"medium","evidence":["biography subject or topic","biography award category"]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[{"name":"Edward Thomas","confidence":0.96},{"name":"Robert Frost","confidence":0.78}],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"France","confidence":0.9}],"argument":{"present":false,"statement":"","confidence":0},"academicOrientation":{"score":35,"confidence":0.55},"profileConfidence":0.78,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralFigures":[{"name":"Rupert Brooke","confidence":0.38}],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"London","confidence":0.45},{"name":"New England","confidence":0.36}],"argument":{"statement":"The book argues that Edward Thomas’s final five years—shaped by his friendship with Robert Frost and his decision to fight—constitute the creative and emotional conditions leading to his tragic death in 1917.","confidence":0.46}},"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"08a3f4033848cdc1d27f568ff6448de07574533245ae0d16feeafc57ec2cc93d","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Biography","secondarySubjects":["Arts & Criticism","War & Military"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book is primarily a biography of Edward Thomas, focusing on his life and work as a poet during a pivotal time, thus fitting it best under Biography, while also addressing his literary influences and the context of World War I.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"a7c8182d0701ea6ff88159aa8429d035107553b7642606d1dace85e9f5ce30db","summary":"Edward Thomas was perhaps the most beguiling and influential of First World War poets. Now All Roads Lead to France is an account of his final five years, centred on his extraordinary friendship with Robert Frost and Thomas's fatal decision to fight in the war. The book also evokes an astonishingly creative moment in English literature, when London was a battleground for new, ambitious kinds of writing. A generation that included W. B. Yeats, Ezra Pound, Robert Frost and Rupert Brooke were 'making it new' - vehemently and pugnaciously. These larger-than-life characters surround a central figure, tormented by his work and his marriage. But as his friendship with Frost blossomed, Thomas wrote poem after poem, and his emotional affliction began to lift. In 1914 the two friends formed the ideas that would produce some of the most remarkable verse of the twentieth century. But the War put an ocean between them: Frost returned to the safety of New England while Thomas stayed to fight for the Old. It is these roads taken - and those not taken - that are at the heart of this remarkable book, which culminates in Thomas's tragic death on Easter Monday 1917.","publicationYear":2011,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"English Poets","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-now-all-roads-lead-to-france-the-last-years-of-edward-thomas-matthew-hollis"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Biography","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-now-all-roads-lead-to-france-the-last-years-of-edward-thomas-matthew-hollis"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Thomas, edward, 1878-1917","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-now-all-roads-lead-to-france-the-last-years-of-edward-thomas-matthew-hollis"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Frost, robert, 1874-1963","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-now-all-roads-lead-to-france-the-last-years-of-edward-thomas-matthew-hollis"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Poets, biography","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-now-all-roads-lead-to-france-th